# TODO - Prepublication hardening

## Objective

Before publication-grade claims, execute one risk-ranked ledger of
nonessential rare inputs and research-evidence failure modes previously
preventing delivery of the real supported C-to-Malbolge path. This is **one**
consolidated task, not dozens of speculative downstream TODOs.

## Deferred inventory

- Uncommon nested malformed CSV/JSON/TOML and numeric representations in the
  opt-in compiler algorithm evaluation arena; unusual error-class identity and
  diagnostic detail combinations; archival study-file rejection permutations.
- Exhaustive retained-raw digest/replay, estimator/budget mismatch and study
  provenance variants not used to accept a current compiler output.
- Pathological source/manifest sizes, deep symlink, filesystem race, Git index
  or hostile host environment situations outside the supported tool path.
- Unsupported CUDA/ROCm/hardware or optional accelerator settings and rare
  synthetic scaling-statistics cases. No physical GPU is a prerequisite.
- Pre-paper stress, reproducibility and adversarial evidence checks that are
  actually required by statements made in the final paper.

## Acceptance

- First demonstrate at least one real supported source input compiled into a
  valid `.malbolge` file that executes with the normative VM, with input/output
  checked independently. Don't claim this proof until it exists.
- Review the deferred list against actual incidents, supported inputs and
  threatened publication claims; promote any safety/correctness-critical case
  immediately instead of deferring it.
- Reuse and preserve existing regression tests. Add missing high-impact negative
  tests, stress evidence, reproducibility metadata and release/citation checks
  only as applicable to supported paths and the intended paper claims.
- Report deferred unavailable hardware as untested; no synthetic success.
- Gate paper-grade claims on applicable complete validation, not core compiler
  development on the entire speculative extreme-case matrix.
